stalled on first append), survive clones, and\n    12\tscope naturally: \"records reachable from this branch\" is a ref walk.\n    13\t\n    14\t## What annals owns, and what it refuses to own\n    15\t\n    16\tannals owns **facts about records**: identity, dedup, ordering, links,\n    17\tcommit anchoring, re-anchoring after squash merges, redaction on write, the\n    18\tpre-push secret scan, and transport. It never interprets `content` — what a\n    19\trecord *means* belongs to the tool that wrote it (`producer.tool`), which\n    20\towns that vocabulary, its versioning, and its reader.\n    21\t\n    22\tA third-party record kind is identified by the pair **`(producer.tool,\n    23\tkind)`**, never by `kind` alone. `producer.version` names the vocabulary\n    24\trevision. annals validates the envelope only; producers validate their own\n    25\tpayloads in their own packages.\n    26\t\n    27\t## The envelope\n    28\t\n    29\t```ts\n    30\t{\n    31\t  id: \"ev2-<sha256 of the identity subset>\",\n    32\t  schema: \"annals/v1\",\n    33\t  kind: string,                    // open; owned by (producer.tool, kind)\n    34\t  occurred_at, recorded_at: string // ISO 8601; recorded_at not in identity\n    35\t  producer: { tool, version? },    // who wrote it; (tool, kind) names a vocabulary\n    36\t  meta?: { ... },                  // producer-owned metadata, uninterpreted\n    37\t  stream?: { id, seq, parent? },   // grouping + ordering, e.g. a conversation\n    38\t  links?: [{ rel, target }],       // typed edges to other records\n    39\t  content: unknown,                // never interpreted by annals\n    40\t  media_type?, raw?, resolved?, redactions?, context?\n    41\t}\n    42\t```\n    43\t\n    44\tThe envelope carries no downstream concepts: no actor, no model, no session.\n    45\tA vocabulary that wants them (cledger records who spoke and which model\n    46\tserved a turn) puts them in `meta`. Each field answers exactly two\n    47\tstore-level questions — is it in the id, and does redaction walk it:\n    48\t\n    49\t| field | in identity | redaction-walked |\n    50\t|---|---|---|\n    51\t| kind, occurred_at, stream, media_type, content, links | yes | content yes |\n    52\t| meta | **no** | **no** — provenance only, never free text |\n    53\t| raw | no | yes (`raw.data`) |\n    54\t| producer, context, recorded_at, redactions | no | no |\n    55\t| resolved | no | **no** — never put payloads here |\n    56\t\n    57\t`meta` being identity-excluded is the point: provenance can be added or\n    58\tenriched later without the same fact getting a new id. It is not\n    59\tredaction-walked — the contract is structured provenance only (ids, names,\n    60\tlabels); anything that could carry source text or a secret belongs in\n    61\t`content`/`raw`. This matches the pre-extraction behavior (actor/producer\n    62\twere never walked) and keeps UUID-heavy metadata out of the entropy scan's\n    63\tfalse-positive path. A vocabulary that\n    64\tneeds one of its facts *in* identity puts it in `content`. Note the standing\n    65\tconsequence: two records differing only by producer or meta dedup to one —\n    66\tbyte-identical content is the same fact.\n    67\t\n    68\t## Namespaces\n    69\t\n    70\tEvery operation takes a `Ledger` — a repo handle plus a namespace — from\n    71\t`openLedger(repo, opts)`. A namespace picks the notes ref\n    72\t(`refs/notes/<name>`), the state directory under `.git`, the config file\n    73\tname, and the CLI the pre-push hook invokes. Producers that want their own\n    74\tindependently-pushable, independently-wipeable ref (a derived layer that must\n    75\tbe rebuildable, say) open their own namespace; producers extending an\n    76\texisting vocabulary write into that vocabulary's namespace through its\n    77\towner's API. cledger uses `{ name: \"conversation-ledger\", configFile: \".cledger.json\" }`.\n    78\t\n    79\tTwo namespaces in one repo chain two hook blocks in `pre-push`. Git feeds\n    80\tthat script its stdin once, so only the first block sees the pushed-ref\n    81\tlist; later blocks degrade to `HEAD` scope — records still push, scoped to\n    82\tthe checked-out branch. And a custom namespace's hook only fires if its\n    83\t`cliName` is on PATH or `hookInvocation` was given; otherwise its owner\n    84\tsyncs through the library.\n    85\t\n    86\t## Size policy\n    87\t\n    88\tRecords carry pointers, digests, and spans — never large artifacts. Git notes\n    89\treplicate to every clone that fetches the ref; a checkpoint, a media file, or\n    90\ta whole database belongs elsewhere, referenced by path + hash (see\n    91\t`resolved`). The redaction stack walks `content` and `raw.data` only, which\n    92\tis the second reason not to smuggle payloads into other fields. Enforced\n    93\tsoftly: an event serializing over `limits.warnEventBytes` (default 5MB)\n    94\twarns on stderr but still appends — capture must never drop records on size\n    95\talone; set `limits.maxEventBytes` to opt into hard refusal for a producer\n    96\tthat would rather fail loudly, such as a derived layer.\n    97\t\n    98\t## Identity records (optional pattern)\n    99\t\n   100\tThe envelope has no actor field, and attribution does not need one: an\n   101\tidentity can be a record like any other — its own kind, its own producer —\n   102\tand a record claims authorship with a link (`{ rel: \"authored_by\", target:\n   103\t<identity record id> }`). That is how git-bug stores identities, versioned\n   104\tand signable, in the same store as its bugs. Nothing in annals mandates\n   105\tthis; a vocabulary can just as well denormalize `meta.actor` onto every\n   106\trecord (cledger does today) and adopt identity records later without a\n   107\tschema change — both are producer-space conventions.\n   108\t\n   109\t## Prior art\n   110\t\n   111\t[git-bug's `entity/dag`](https://github.com/git-bug/git-bug) (Go) is the\n   112\tclosest relative — replicated append-only operations in git refs, id = hash\n   113\tof the serialized op, per-type `{namespace, typename, formatVersion}`; its\n   114\tidentification triple matches annals' `(producer.tool, kind,\n   115\tproducer.version)`. Dolt's git-remote support solves an overlapping problem\n   116\twith a versioned SQL database; it lacks commit anchoring, which is the\n   117\tmechanism everything here hangs off.\n     1\t#!/usr/bin/env node\n     2\t/**\n     3\t * Minimal CLI for the default `annals` namespace: the two commands transport\n     4\t * needs to exist (`transport-push` for the pre-push hook, `sync` for manual\n     5\t * use). Vocabulary owners (cledger, …) ship their own CLIs for their own\n     6\t * namespaces; this one exists so a bare `annals` install is self-contained.\n     7\t */\n     8\timport { findRepo } from \"./git.js\";\n     9\timport { openLedger } from \"./ledger.js\";\n    10\timport { parsePrePushRefs, ScanBlockedError, sync, transportPush } from \"./store.js\";\n    11\t\n    12\tasync function readStdin(): Promise<string> {\n    13\t  const chunks: Buffer[] = [];\n    14\t  for await (const chunk of process.stdin) chunks.push(chunk as Buffer);\n    15\t  return Buffer.concat(chunks).toString(\"utf8\");\n    16\t}\n    17\t\n    18\tasync function requireLedger() {\n    19\t  const repo = await findRepo(process.cwd());\n    20\t  if (!repo) {\n    21\t    process.stderr.write(\"annals: not inside a git repository\\n\");\n    22\t    process.exit(2);\n    23\t  }\n    24\t  return openLedger(repo);\n    25\t}\n    26\t\n    27\tasync function cmdTransportPush(positional: string[]): Promise<void> {\n    28\t  const repo = await findRepo(process.cwd());\n    29\t  if (!repo) return; // a hook must never fail the user's push\n    30\t  const ledger = openLedger(repo);\n    31\t  const remote = positional[0] || \"origin\";\n    32\t  // git's pre-push hook pipes the refs being pushed. Only read whe